o choose. Maybe the knight on g8's move to f6 is also good. Let's see if there's a reason to prefer one. \n\nLooking at the position, the king is in the center, so castling would be a future consideration. If I develop the knight on g8 to f6, it would be closer to the kingside, which might support castling. But since it's early, perhaps both moves are equally good. \n\nIn the absence of any immediate threats, developing either knight is fine. I'll go with N: b8-c6 as the first move. Alternatively, N: g8-f6. But since both are valid, maybe the user expects one. Let me check if there's any reason to prefer one. \n\nWait, the valid moves list includes both knights. The user hasn't specified any constraints beyond the move list. So choosing either is acceptable. But perhaps the first knight move is more standard. \n\nAlternatively, maybe moving the knight on g8 to f6 is better because it's closer to the center. But both moves are to central squares. \n\nIn many opening principles, it's recommended to develop knights before bishops, but here the bishops are still on the board. However, the valid moves don't include bishop moves because the pawns are blocking. So pawn moves or knight moves are the options. \n\nSince the user wants the best move, and both knight moves are equally good, I'll pick one. Let's go with N: b8-c6. That's a common developing move.\n</think>\n\nEvaluation: Developing the knight to c6 controls the center and prepares for castling, aligning with standard opening principles.  \nReason: The knight move N: b8-c6 activates a piece, exerts central pressure, and is a common early development choice.  \nAnswer: N: b8-c6"}
